WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 315: Elizabeth I (Part 1): The Virgin Queen, the Tilbury Speech, the Spanish Armada, and the Golden Age

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.040
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series begins a grand two-part chronicle of England's greatest queen.

4
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:20.800
[Rebecca] A ruler who steered her nation through religious peril, defeated the mightiest empire on earth, and inspired an immortal golden age.

5
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:29.040
[Rebecca] Queen Elizabeth, I hear is George with Part 1: The Virgin Queen and the Spanish Armada.

6
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:40.800
[George] Born in September 1533 at Greenwich Palace to Henry VIII and Anne Bolin, Elizabeth's life began as a bitter disappointment to her father, who had desperately craved a son.

7
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:49.200
[George] When her mother was beheaded when Elizabeth was barely two, she was declared an illegitimate bastard and raised in quiet country manners.

8
00:00:49.200 --> 00:01:00.320
[George] Blessed with brilliant intelligence, Elizabeth mastered Greek, Latin, French, and Italian under humanists like Roger Asham, developing a cautious, watchful cunning.

9
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.200
[George] Her survival during the turbulent reigns of Edward V.

10
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.960
[George] I and Mary I was a miraculous feat of political agility.

11
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:24.800
[George] Implicated in Wyatt's rebellion in 1554, 20-year-old Elizabeth was arrested and brought to the Tower of London by water, stepping ashore at Traitor's Gate in the pouring rain, declaring, here landeth as true a subject, being prisoner as ever landed at these stairs.

12
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:31.040
[George] Released into house arrest at Woodstock, she deflected every trap set by Catholic interrogators.

13
00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:44.000
[George] On November 17, 1558, when messengers galloped into the park of Hatfield House to inform her that Mary was dead, 25-year-old Elizabeth was sitting under an oak tree reading her Greek Bible.

14
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.520
[George] Falling to her knees on the frozen grass, she quoted the 118th Psalm in Latin.

15
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.720
[George] A domino factum est istud, et est muribile in Oculies Nostris.

16
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.320
[George] It is the Lord's doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes.

17
00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:05.960
[George] During her perilous months of confinement in the Tower under Mary, Elizabeth lived under the constant shadow of the executioner's axe.

18
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:15.720
[George] Her rooms in the Bell Tower were cold and damp, and she was interrogated relentlessly by Bishop Gardiner, who sought any pretext to link her to Wyatt's treason.

19
00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.280
[George] Her legendary survival lay in her razor-sharp linguistic ambiguity.

20
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:29.720
[George] When pressed to state her theological belief regarding the real presence of Christ in the communion bread, Elizabeth famously composed her immortal four-line riddle.

21
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:35.640
[George] Christ was the word that spake it, he took the bread and break it, and what his word did make it.

22
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.560
[George] That I believe and take it.

23
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:44.520
[George] The baffled interrogators could find no heresy in her words, preserving her life until Mary's final passing.

24
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:51.240
[George] Elizabeth inherited a kingdom bankrupt, defeated abroad, and violently divided by religious faction.

25
00:02:51.240 --> 00:03:04.440
[George] With exquisite political pragmatism, the young Queen crafted the Elizabethan religious settlement of 1559, steering England between the extremes of Roman Catholicism and radical Calvinist Puritanism.

26
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:18.120
[George] The act of supremacy restored the Crown's authority over the Church, though Elizabeth astutely adopted the title Supreme Governor, rather than Supreme Head, assuaging conservative Catholics who held that Christ alone was head of the Church.

27
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:30.760
[George] The act of uniformity reinstated Cranmer's Book of Common Prayer, but with deliberate ambiguities in the communion service that allowed both Catholics and Protestants to interpret the sacrament according to their consciences.

28
00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:40.600
[George] Elizabeth famously declared that she had no desire to make windows into men's souls, asking only for outward civic conformity and attendance at parish churches.

29
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:56.240
[George] This pragmatic via media, the middle way, diffused the sectarian fires that were consuming continental Europe in religious wars, providing thirty years of stable domestic peace that allowed English commerce, culture, and national identity to flourish.

30
00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:05.840
[George] The Elizabethan religious settlement of 1559 was an extraordinary political triumph of moderation orchestrated by Elizabeth and Sir William Cecil.

31
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:16.160
[George] By defining the Crown as supreme governor rather than supreme head, Elizabeth accommodated conservative clergy who balked at a woman claiming headship over Christ's church.

32
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:30.080
[George] She deliberately preserved cathedral choirs, organs, clerical vestments, and traditional episcopal hierarchies, refusing the radical demands of returning Genevan Marian exiles to abolish bishops and strip churches bare.

33
00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:46.240
[George] Her settlement created the enduring liturgical identity of the Church of England, Catholic in structure and ceremony, yet Protestant in theology and vernacular scripture, providing a comprehensive national umbrella that accommodated the vast majority of her subjects.

34
00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:55.200
[George] From the moment of her accession, Parliament and her council, led by the astute William Cecil, badgered Elizabeth to marry and secure the succession.

35
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.280
[George] Suitors flocked from every royal court in Europe King Philip I.

36
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:06.240
[George] of Spain, Archduke Charles of Austria, and the French princes Henry of Anjou and Francis of Alenson.

37
00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:19.840
[George] Elizabeth turned her marriage negotiations into a brilliant diplomatic instrument, flirting, delaying, and playing rival European superpowers against one another for twenty five years without ever surrendering English independence.

38
00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:29.120
[George] Her heart, however, belonged to one man, Robert Dudley, Earl of Leicester, a dashing, dark-eyed courtier who had shared her imprisonment in the tower.

39
00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:40.520
[George] But when Dudley's wife, Amy Robsart, was found dead at the bottom of a staircase in 1560 with a broken neck, murder scandals erupted that made marriage politically impossible.

40
00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:57.320
[George] Elizabeth declared to Parliament that she was already married to a husband, the realm of England, displaying her coronation ring, asserting that it would be sufficient for her that a marble stone declare a queen, having reigned such a time, lived and died a virgin.

41
00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:11.320
[George] The tragic death of Amy Robsart at Cumner Place in September 1560 remains one of history's greatest unsolved mysteries, found with a broken neck at the foot of a short flight of stairs while her servants were away at the Abingdon Fair.

42
00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:15.880
[George] Rumors exploded that Robert Dudley had murdered his wife to marry the Queen.

43
00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:27.240
[George] Though the coroner's inquest returned a verdict of accidental death, likely caused by metastatic breast cancer weakening her cervical vertebrae, the political scandal made marriage impossible.

44
00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:32.600
[George] William Cecil warned Elizabeth that if she married Dudley, the realm would rebel.

45
00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:45.320
[George] Elizabeth chose England over her heart, keeping Dudley as her lifelong master of the horse and favourite, affectionately calling him her eyes and nursing his portrait in a secret bedside cabinet until her own death.

46
00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:48.440
[George] While Elizabeth maintained formal peace with Philip I.

47
00:06:53.880 --> 00:07:05.000
[George] English privateers, known as the Sea Dogs, men like John Hawkins and Francis Drake, raided Spanish treasure fleets and Caribbean settlements with the Queen's secret investment.

48
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:21.440
[George] Between 1577 and 1580, Francis Drake sailed aboard the Golden Hind on an epic circumnavigation of the globe, raiding Spanish silver ports in Chile and Peru and capturing the giant treasure galleon Nustra Senora de la Concepcion.

49
00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:28.720
[George] Returning to Dipped Ford with gold and silver that yielded a staggering 4,700% return on investment.

50
00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:34.880
[George] Elizabeth boarded Drake's ship and knighted him on his own quarter deck, infuriating King Philip.

51
00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:51.520
[George] In 1587, when Philip began assembling a colossal invasion fleet in Cadiz, Drake led 23 English warships directly into Cadiz Harbour in an audacious raid, destroying 37 Spanish ships and burning millions of staves of barrel timber.

52
00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:59.120
[George] An operation Drake merrily dubbed, singing the King of Spain's beard, delaying the Spanish invasion by a full year.

53
00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:06.640
[George] Francis Drake's circumnavigation aboard the Golden Hind was a maritime odyssey that shattered the myth of Spanish ocean supremacy.

54
00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:17.520
[George] Navigating the treacherous, frozen straits of Magellan, Drake burst into the Pacific Ocean, raiding Spanish treasure ports in Chile and Peru that had never seen an enemy warship.

55
00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:34.800
[George] When Drake returned to Plymouth in September 1580, ballasted with 26 tons of silver bullion and 80 pounds of gold, Elizabeth boarded the vessel at Dipped Ford, banqueting in his cabin before commanding the French ambassador to hand her a golden sword with which she knighted Drake.

56
00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.440
[George] Openly defying King Philip's furious demands for the pirates' execution.

57
00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:49.760
[George] In May 1588, Philip II's great and most fortunate navy, the Invincible Armada, set sail from Lisbon under the Duke of Medina Sidonia.

58
00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.800
[George] One hundred and thirty massive warships carrying 30,000 soldiers and sailors.

59
00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:00.440
[George] Intended to rendezvous in Flanders with the Duke of Parma's veteran army and invade England.

60
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:07.240
[George] On July 19, 1588, English beacon fires blazed along the cliffs from the Lizard to London.

61
00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:14.200
[George] The armada was sighted entering the English Channel in a formidable crescent formation spanning seven miles across.

62
00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:28.280
[George] Commanded by Charles Howard of Effingham, Francis Drake, and John Hawkins, the English fleet deployed newly designed race-built galleons that were lower, faster, and more manoeuvrable than the topheavy Spanish floating castles.

63
00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:40.440
[George] For a week, as the armada moved up the channel, English ships shadowed the Spaniards, staying upwind and pounding them with long-range culverin cannon fire while refusing to close for boarding actions.

64
00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:48.760
[George] On August 6, Medina Sidonia anchored his fleet off Calais, awaiting Parma's troops, vulnerable in the open roadstead.

65
00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:54.520
[George] Philip II's Armada enterprise was motivated by religious crusade and imperial vengeance.

66
00:09:54.520 --> 00:10:03.320
[George] Pope Sixtus V had issued a papal bull blessing the invasion and offering plenary indulgences to every soldier who sailed against the heretic Queen.

67
00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:13.240
[George] Medina Sidonia's massive floating fortresses carried 300,000 cannonballs, and over a hundred and eighty friars and inquisitors prepared to reconvert England.

68
00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:18.680
[George] But Spanish naval doctrine relied on close-range grappling and infantry boarding actions.

69
00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:34.120
[George] English shipwright John Hawkins had revolutionized the English Navy with lower, sleeker hulls and heavy long range culverin cannon mounted on four wheeled truck carriages, allowing English gun crews to reload three times faster than their Spanish counterparts.

70
00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:41.320
[George] With the Spanish invasion fleet anchored just 20 miles across the Straits of Dover, England braced for land invasion.

71
00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:48.160
[George] At Tilbury on the Thames estuary, an English land army of twenty thousand militia gathered under the Earl of Leicester.

72
00:10:48.400 --> 00:11:03.280
[George] On August 9, 1588, Queen Elizabeth rode into the military camp on a white gelding, wearing a polished silver cuirass over a white velvet dress, carrying a silver marshal's truncheon in her hand, with no armed bodyguard.

73
00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:11.680
[George] Before thousands of cheering soldiers, the fifty-four-year-old queen delivered what became the most iconic royal address in English history.

74
00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:20.720
[George] My loving people, we have been persuaded by some that are careful of our safety to take heed how we commit ourselves to armed multitudes, for fear of treachery.

75
00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:26.080
[George] But I assure you, I do not desire to live to distrust my faithful and loving people.

76
00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:40.800
[George] I know I have the body but of a weak and feeble woman, but I have the heart and stomach of a king, and of a king of England too, and think foul scorn that Parma or Spain, or any prince of Europe, should dare to invade the borders of my realm.

77
00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:46.880
[George] Her electrifying defiance bound sovereign and subjects into an unbreakable spiritual brotherhood.

78
00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:52.720
[George] The scene at Tilbury on August 9 was an unforgettable spectacle of Renaissance Royal Majesty.

79
00:11:52.720 --> 00:12:04.240
[George] Elizabeth rode among her soldiers accompanied only by Leicester, the Earl of Ormond carrying the sword of state before her, and a page bearing her white-plumed silver helmet on a velvet cushion.

80
00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:11.360
[George] Her speech was written down on the spot by royal chaplain Lionel Sharp, who read it aloud to the assembled regiments.

81
00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:25.680
[George] When Elizabeth declared, I myself will take up arms, I myself will be your general, judge, and rewarder of every one of your virtues in the field, hardened yeomen weeped with devotion, shouting their readiness to die for Gloriana.

82
00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:30.760
[George] That midnight off Calais, Drake executed a masterstroke of tactical warfare.

83
00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:40.360
[George] Eight English warships packed with pitch, brimstone, and loaded cannon were set ablaze and drifted on the tide straight toward the tightly anchored Spanish armada.

84
00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:46.440
[George] In terror, Spanish captains cut their anchor cables, scattering into the dark North Sea.

85
00:12:46.440 --> 00:13:01.480
[George] At dawn on August 8, at the Battle of Gravelines, English ships closed in, raking the disorganized Spanish galleons at point blank range with broadsides, sinking several warships and driving the armada onto the treacherous Flemish shoals.

86
00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:11.720
[George] Suddenly, a ferocious gale hailed across England as the Protestant wind blew from the southwest, pushing the battered Spanish fleet into the stormy North Sea.

87
00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:20.680
[George] Unable to beat back against the wind, Medina Sidonia had no choice but to sail around the rugged coasts of Scotland and Ireland to return home.

88
00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:26.360
[George] Autumn storms wrecked dozens of Spanish galleons on the razor reefs of the Hebrides and Connet.

89
00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:30.520
[George] Fewer than half the ships and ten thousand men limped back to Spain.

90
00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:38.920
[George] Elizabeth struck a victory medal bearing the Latin inscription, Flavit et Dissipatis Sunt, God blew, and they were scattered.

91
00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:42.280
[George] England was saved, and Gloriana was immortal.

92
00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:46.520
[George] The wreckage of the armada was an environmental and human cataclysm.

93
00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:55.640
[George] Battered by unseasonal North Atlantic gales, dozens of Spanish galleons shattered on the jagged granite reefs of the Outer Hebrides and County Clare.

94
00:13:55.640 --> 00:14:04.600
[George] Starving Spanish survivors who washed ashore on the Irish coast were summarily butchered by English garrisons or stripped of their clothing by Gaelic clansmen.

95
00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:05.440
[George] Philip I.

96
00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:12.200
[George] I wept in his private cell at the Escorial Palace, murmuring, I sent my ships to fight against men.

97
00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:13.880
[George] Not against the seas.

98
00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:25.920
[George] The victory established England as an invincible maritime power, ignited an explosion of national self-confidence, and ensured that the Protestant Reformation would survive in Northern Europe.

99
00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:30.320
[Rebecca] Thank you for joining us for this episode of Most Fascinating Stories.

100
00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:46.880
[Rebecca] If you enjoyed this chapter of Elizabeth I Part 1, the Virgin Queen, the Tilbury Speech, the Spanish Armada, and the Golden Age, subscribe to the show, share it with a fellow history lover, and tune in next Tuesday for our next journey into history's most extraordinary events.

101
00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:49.440
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

